                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Maze/time attack obstacle course where you’re guiding a, um, “fly”, right? through a hundred black and white Unity maps racing against time constraints that are [b]laughably[/b] tight. Yeah—you’re not getting past stage 20: the hit boxes are garbage, and the difficulty feels like one of the sliders broke off. You’ll swear you’re missing an input control after stage 2, but nope! All you need is your D-pad, stick, or arrow keys. Pfft. \n\nAnd my Steam library tagging suggests that I completed this?, which, just… no, I didn’t. Well, I’m not bothering to figure out how or why I believed that to be the case, anyway. \n\nTL;DR [i]Utterly impossible[/i] and unpleasant “casual” indie dreck with fake reviews and zero historical player base. \n\n[b]EDIT[/b] Dammit, I think I did beat this. Going back one last time, you literally have to hold the Up arrow while directing your \"little furry friend\" with your controller to beat even the first 10 stages. No one play tested this. Nobody's coming to save us.\n\n[b]Pros:[/b]\n[list]\n[*] Use secret code “Escape key + Escape key” in-game to access the next part of your day\n[/list]\n[b]Cons:[/b]\n[list]\n[*] Almost funny as a punishment\n[*] For $4.99, buy a brick, smash one of your left toes, and use the change to buy a bottle of tabasco sauce to pour into your next hangnail: it’ll be a better story and a better time than this",
